<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- agent-infra-plist-version: 0.2.0 -->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- ai-multi-repo: a SECOND orchestrator controller, bound to another repo
         (scripts/orchestrator-harness/multi-repo-controller.sh).

         ⛔ WHY (measured 2026-10-23). The running controller is bound to ONE repo, and the
         harness's PR path (pr-doctor / objective / lander) only ever sees the repo its
         controller is bound to — so `agent-infra`'s ~70 open PRs received no automatic
         dispatch at all. agent-infra #2281 ("a machine that runs SEVERAL repos needs one
         dispatch…") is the real fix; this job is the workaround: a second controller with
         its OWN state dir, so the two never share a lock, a candidate list or a worker
         registry.

         ⛔ SHIPS UNSCHEDULED BY DEFAULT. install-launchd.sh installs every template here
         with no arguments, and pi-bootstrap/setup.sh runs exactly that on every Darwin box.
         A second CONTROLLER on every box would double-dispatch every queue in the fleet, so
         the three identity values are ENV-ONLY placeholders with no defaults (unset → the
         template is SKIPPED with a loud warning). Activate deliberately, on one box, for one
         repo:
           AI_MULTI_REPO_SLUG=daniel-ospina/agent-infra \
           AI_MULTI_REPO_REPO=$HOME/agent-infra-main \
           AI_MULTI_REPO_STATE=$HOME/orchestrator-ai \
             bash scripts/install-launchd.sh
         then create that controller's identity ONCE (state dir + its own pin file):
           OR=$AI_MULTI_REPO_SLUG REPO=$AI_MULTI_REPO_REPO O=$AI_MULTI_REPO_STATE \
             HARNESS_PIN=$AI_MULTI_REPO_STATE/state/HARNESS-PIN \
             bash scripts/orchestrator-harness/multi-repo-controller.sh, init mode

         ⛔ THE LAUNCHER REFUSES THE SILENT-SHARING FAILURES BY NAME, so this job cannot
         quietly become a second owner of the FIRST controller's identity:
           * O equal to the primary's state dir ($HOME/orchestrator) → refused (two
             controllers on one $O fight over .orch.lock, candidates.txt and the registry);
           * HARNESS_PIN equal to the primary's pin, or absent → refused (two controllers
             advancing one pin rewrite each other's harness provenance);
           * state dir absent → refused, with the init remedy;
           * REPO not a git work tree, OR not owner/name → refused.
         That is also why the launchd job is KeepAlive=false with RunAtLoad=false: the
         refusal is a LOUD refusal, not a restart loop that hides it. `launchctl kickstart`
         starts it; `orchestrator-bringup.sh status` reports whether it is cycling.

         ⛔ IT CARRIES A REAL SCHEDULE NOW, AND "no StartInterval" WAS THE DEFECT (agent-infra#2908,
         measured 2026-10-27). Without one, launchd accepts the plist silently and the job
         simply NEVER RUNS — and a Linux cell's runtime refuses the whole LAYER over it
         (`fleet-schedule.py`: "a job with no schedule would never run"), which is how this
         template took cell4's scheduler down together with ai-drainer. 900 s is chosen for
         the TOP-UP semantics, not for a sweep: the launcher `exec`s orch-loop.sh, which
         takes $O/.orch.lock and EXITS 0 at once when that lock names a live pid
         (orch-loop.sh:1526). So a tick can only ever REFILL a dead second controller, never
         start a competing one — which is exactly why an interval is safe here while
         KeepAlive would be wrong: this stays a loud refusal that is retried, not a restart
         loop that hides it. 900 s bounds how long a dead second controller goes unnoticed at
         three times the primary `controller-watchdog`'s 5-minute poll, and each tick costs one
         lock read when the controller is healthy.

         ⛔ ON A LINUX CELL THIS JOB IS REFUSED BY NAME, NOT KILLED ON A CLOCK. Its three identity
         placeholders are unset there, so it is already a named SKIP for that reason; if an operator
         ever SETS them, `FleetResident` below (a fleet key this runtime reads; launchd ignores keys
         it does not know — measured 2026-10-27: such a plist bootstraps rc=0) makes it a named SKIP
         on the cell a second time, whatever its StartInterval says. That is the code enforcement of
         the distinction this paragraph used to only describe: the tick loop supervises nothing, so
         it would start the launcher, the launcher would exec a controller that never returns, and
         the loop would kill it at FLEET_SCHEDULE_JOB_TIMEOUT (1800 s) — a controller restarted every
         ceiling, not a service. On the Mac the StartInterval is still the top-up described above;
         a resident second controller on a CELL belongs to the cell's own supervisor.
         ⛔ WHY A DECLARATION AND NOT A GUESS. The loop cannot tell whether a program returns, so
         "is this body resident" is only knowable from the plist. `KeepAlive` would be WRONG here
         (launchd would restart it in a loop that hides the launcher's loud refusals), and this file
         still needs its 900 s top-up, so the declaration had to be a third thing: FleetResident.

         ⛔ THE DECISION THE CONTROLLER MAKES PER CYCLE IS NOT THIS PLIST'S. HEARTBEAT is
         read from {{AI_MULTI_REPO_STATE}}/heartbeat by the inventory row, which is the only
         source of "alive and cycling" — a loaded job with no fresh heartbeat is LATE, not
         healthy (scripts/lib/fleet-inventory.sh, row `second-controller`).

         Rendered by scripts/install-launchd.sh (#304) — placeholders only. -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.eldato.ai-multi-repo</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>{{AGENT_INFRA_PATH}}/scripts/orchestrator-harness/multi-repo-controller.sh</string>
    </array>
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>{{PATH}}</string>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>{{HOME}}</string>
        <key>OR</key>
        <string>{{AI_MULTI_REPO_SLUG}}</string>
        <key>REPO</key>
        <string>{{AI_MULTI_REPO_REPO}}</string>
        <key>O</key>
        <string>{{AI_MULTI_REPO_STATE}}</string>
        <key>HARNESS_PIN</key>
        <string>{{AI_MULTI_REPO_STATE}}/state/HARNESS-PIN</string>
        <key>HARNESS_REPO</key>
        <string>{{AI_MULTI_REPO_REPO}}</string>
        <key>REVIEW_GATE</key>
        <string>pipeline-compliance</string>
        <key>REQ</key>
        <string>pipeline-compliance</string>
    </dict>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <false/>
    <!-- ⛔ THE BODY IS A RESIDENT SERVICE, EVEN THOUGH THIS PLIST SCHEDULES IT. The launcher execs
         orch-loop.sh, which never returns — so on a host with no supervisor (a Linux cell) this job
         must be REFUSED by `fleet-schedule.py`, not started-and-killed at its 1800 s ceiling. This
         key is the declaration that makes that a named SKIP; launchd ignores it (measured
         2026-10-27). Do not replace it with KeepAlive: that would make launchd restart the job in
         a loop, which is the one thing this template deliberately does not do. -->
    <key>FleetResident</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- 900 s: a top-up, not a sweep — see the note above. -->
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>900</integer>
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/tmp/ai-multi-repo.launchd.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/tmp/ai-multi-repo.launchd.err</string>
</dict>
</plist>
